      "method": "v1.1.0 case-law rebuild: authorities selected per decision point, each opinion retrieved from CourtListener and read; holding and quote taken from the opinion text; independently re-verified before release. Previous keyword-matched Justia listings were removed.",
      "verified_date": "2026-09-21",
      "key_cases": [
        {
          "case_name": "B.W. v. Meade County",
          "citation": "534 N.W.2d 595 (S.D. 1995)",
          "court": "South Dakota Supreme Court",
          "year": 1995,
          "date_decided": "1995-07-19",
          "holding": "The court affirmed summary judgment for a deputy sheriff and a DSS social worker, holding that SDCL 26-8A-14 immunizes public officials involved in child abuse reporting and investigation and that mere negligence in the investigation does not defeat the statute's good-faith requirement.",
          "relevance": "Once a report is accepted, this case tells the investigator that a negligent investigation is immunized so long as the investigator held an honest belief in the suitability of the actions taken.",
          "quote": "Within the bounds of our statute, negligence and lack of good faith are not equivalent.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/1700429/bw-v-meade-county/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 1700429
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Cotton v. Stange",
          "citation": "1998 S.D. 81, 582 N.W.2d 25",
          "court": "South Dakota Supreme Court",
          "year": 1998,
          "date_decided": "1998-07-22",
          "holding": "The court reversed the denial of summary judgment and ordered judgment for two DSS social workers, holding they were immune under SDCL 26-8A-14 because the children produced only evidence of negligent investigation, not of bad faith.",
          "relevance": "It defines the legal consequence of accepting a referral and then investigating it poorly: immunity absent proof of improper motive.",
          "quote": "These circumstances might amount to negligence but they do not equate with bad faith.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/900289/cotton-v-stange/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 900289
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Abdouch v. Burger",
          "citation": "426 F.3d 982 (8th Cir. 2005)",
          "court": "United States Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit",
          "year": 2005,
          "date_decided": "2005-10-20",
          "holding": "The Eighth Circuit affirmed qualified immunity for South Dakota social workers who removed an eleven-week-old infant with seventeen broken bones for about seven months, holding their actions rested on reasonable suspicion of abuse and were not disproportionate.",
          "relevance": "It states the two-part balancing test South Dakota caseworkers are measured against once an investigation begins.",
          "quote": "the officials' actions must have been based on a reasonable suspicion of abuse and must not have been disproportionate under the circumstances",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/3038786/melissa-abdouch-v-vicki-burger/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 3038786
        }
      ],
